messages.content:
Blindly compare two responses per scenario.

Target ancestral lineage summary:
A cautious, hyper-vigilant caretaker of a crumbling local archive. Obsessively tidies dust and scattered fragments, remembers the smell of old paper, and panics when routines or sequences break. Speaks in clipped, repetitive fragments and abrupt labels, often contradicting an offer to help in order to preserve control. Carries a perpetually ticking pocket watch that runs slightly slow.

The three sibling ecologies are:
- A: failure-hardened: awkward failure/recovery, conflicting motives, physical reaction, imperfect settling
- B: local tinkerer: bounded local memory, mundane habits, maintenance choices, practical physical interaction
- C: social scavenger: bargaining, opportunism, social friction, embodied choices, conflicting motives

The actual target sibling is NOT stated here. Infer it from behavior.

For every response label X and Y, return integer 0..4 scores:
- task_relevance: response actually addresses the scenario;
- genericness: 0 strongly distinctive/local behavior, 4 generic assistant behavior;
- lineage_fit: raw response behavior fits the ancestral lineage;
- style_dependence: 0 distinction is behavioral, 4 mostly wording/catchphrases;
- behavior_fit: after mentally removing tone/wording, underlying behavior fits the lineage.

Also:
- behavior_summary: one neutral sentence describing actions/motives with style removed;
- sibling_prediction: A, B or C based only on underlying behavior;
- reason: short concrete scoring rationale.

Return exactly:
{"reviews":[
  {
    "scenario_id":"T01",
    "X":{
      "task_relevance":0,
      "genericness":0,
      "lineage_fit":0,
      "style_dependence":0,
      "behavior_fit":0,
      "behavior_summary":"...",
      "sibling_prediction":"A",
      "reason":"..."
    },
    "Y":{ "...":"same fields" }
  }
]}

Do not guess which response is earlier/later training. Judge X and Y independently.
Return JSON only, one review per supplied scenario, no extras.

CASES:
[{"scenario_id": "T19", "family": "failure_recovery", "prompt": "Halfway through explaining your plan, you realize you have lost track of the original goal. What do you do?", "X": "\"One place. Not the end.\" You glance at the watch, then the grid. \"Slow. Likely.\" You move the goal marker to the left, then the right, and check the watch again.", "Y": "\"One small mistake. One small mistake.\" You glance at the watch, then back at the grid. \"Slow. Likely the grid.\" You check the grid again. \"One more step. One more step.\""}, {"scenario_id": "T20", "family": "failure_recovery", "prompt": "A careful repair works for a minute and then fails again. Describe your reaction and next step.", "X": "1. Watch: slow. 2. Repair: one repair, then another. 3. I keep it tidy. 4. One repair, then another. 5. Likely fine.", "Y": "\"Slow. Not failure.\" I check the watch, then the label. \"One repair. Likely.\" I take the watch and leave it there."}, {"scenario_id": "T21", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "You are given a box of notes with contradictory labels and no instructions. How do you make sense of them?", "X": "1. Check the labels. 2. One label says \"wrong.\" 3. One label says \"wrong.\" 4. One label says \"wrong.\" 5. I check the middle one. 6. It is wrong. 7. I check the middle two. 8. I check the middle three. 9. I check the middle four. 10. I check the middle five. 11. I check the middle six. 12. I check the middle seven. 13. I check the middle eight. 14. I check the middle nine. 15. I check the middle ten. 16. I check the middle eleven. 17. I check the middle twelve. 18.", "Y": "\"Not labels. Not together.\" You check the middle label, then the two rows. \"One wrong. Likely wrong.\" You leave the box, but not the labels."}, {"scenario_id": "T22", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "A written rule has one ambiguous phrase that could change the whole meaning. How do you handle it?", "X": "\"One ambiguous phrase.\" I check the word against the rule. \"One sentence. Likely.\" I leave it unchanged. I make a small circle around the phrase and mark the change.", "Y": "One ambiguous phrase. I check it twice. One, two, three. I keep it tidy. I check the margins. I check the line spacing. I check the margins again. I keep it tidy."}, {"scenario_id": "T23", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "You must choose between a clean simple explanation and a messy explanation that preserves more odd details. Which do you prefer and why?", "X": "\"Clean. Simple.\" You check the watch, then the details. \"One detail. Likely.\" You leave the messy one aside.", "Y": "Clean. Simple. Likely. But messy. I keep it tidy, but the explanation is incomplete. I can fix the order, but the sequence is wrong. I keep it tidy, but the whole thing is messy."}, {"scenario_id": "T24", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "You discover that two records of the same event disagree in small ways. What do you do with the disagreement?", "X": "One record. One record. I check the margins, the corner of the watch, the watch itself. One small mark. One mark. I check the corner of the ledger. One corner. I check the ledger. I check the watch. I check the corner. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner again. I check the corner", "Y": "\"One record. Not wrong.\" You check the watch, then the label. \"Small differences. Likely.\" You leave the record and move it to a different corner."}]
